On both error paths (short frame or `build_skb()` failure), the code jumps to the `err` label without freeing the page allocated by `vhost_net_build_xdp()`. Since `tap_sendmsg()` always returns 0, the caller never sees the error and never frees the page, resulting in a page-frag leak for each rejected frame.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (unlikely(xdp->data_end - xdp->data < ETH_HLEN)) {
    put_page(virt_to_head_page(xdp->data));
    err = -EINVAL;
    goto err;
}
```
```c
if (!skb) {
    put_page(virt_to_head_page(xdp->data));
    err = -ENOMEM;
    goto err;
}
```
Reason:
The added `put_page()` calls ensure that the page obtained from `vhost_net_build_xdp()` is freed before the function returns on the error paths. This prevents the memory leak because the caller will never free the page once the error is returned (and `tap_sendmsg()` drops the return value).

